  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, Q3: the interval clock is FIXED RATE -- `each successive interval arrives n ticks after the previous interval, on that original schedule. The period is never re-measured from the moment an invocation happens to finish` (spec/interaction-events.md:182-186). `every 2` with a body that takes 3 ticks overruns its own interval on every invocation, so the two readings separate immediately. Measured: the intervals stand at ticks 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10 no matter when a body ends, and the outer `wait 4`'s four checkpoints -- ticks 1, 2, 6 and 10, because each body's nested `wait 3` advances the shared clock -- fire the handler THREE times. Measured against a runtime mutated to the rejected fixed-DELAY reading, where each period is disarmed for the duration of an invocation and re-armed from its completion tick, the same program fires it only TWICE. Fixed delay also makes the queueing sentence at :188-195 dead text, since a new interval could never arrive while one was running -- which is the argument that settles it.",
